{"h":3395,"lines":[{"y":0.069767441720357204,"t":"Test 7","x":0.098765432117169968,"w":0.061728394826253266,"h":0.013081395583944722,"c":1},{"w":0.3086419677734375,"y":0.1162790696315219,"h":0.01453488398216074,"t":"READING PASSAGE 3","c":1,"x":0.098765435771747526},{"t":"You should spend about 20 minutes on Questions 27-40, which are based on Reading","y":0.15106007058556981,"h":0.019140164899882239,"x":0.098765433108713024,"c":1,"w":0.82716049194335939},{"c":1,"t":"Passage 3.","x":0.098688358152461603,"y":0.17123990470422057,"h":0.016531817904630763,"w":0.11126525878906249},{"x":0.35185185628447274,"y":0.2093023254180999,"h":0.017441860778592999,"c":1,"w":0.33127571105957027,"t":"Music and the emotions"},{"x":0.19341564897807018,"y":0.24705535913240217,"c":1,"h":0.017479524735988794,"w":0.65020573933919268,"t":"Neuroscientist Jonah Lehrer considers the emotional power of music"},{"h":0.019493988180890653,"w":0.80256006876627617,"c":1,"t":"Why does music make us feel? On the one hand, music is a purely abstract art form,","x":0.098713528453505714,"y":0.28146079132059687},{"c":1,"w":0.76154032389322912,"y":0.29921219648371422,"h":0.020802804522295215,"x":0.098671409918047265,"t":"devoid of language or explicit ideas. And yet, even though music says little, it still"},{"y":0.3163456260135078,"h":0.021984760837083117,"t":"manages to touch us deeply. When listening to our favourite songs, our body betrays all","x":0.096601451677638298,"w":0.83150512695312484,"c":1},{"c":1,"t":"the symptoms of emotional arousal. The pupils in our eyes dilate, our pulse and blood","w":0.81275721232096343,"x":0.096707813566541456,"h":0.017508628652008751,"y":0.33568904591945836},{"y":0.35250059382146959,"w":0.80670842488606764,"c":1,"x":0.098699256329400006,"h":0.01988856334708744,"t":"pressure rise, the electrical conductance of our skin is lowered, and the cerebellum, a"},{"x":0.098696381643431058,"c":1,"w":0.82730636596679685,"y":0.3704063025804003,"t":"brain region associated with bodily movement, becomes strangely active. Blood is even","h":0.020555247968442325},{"x":0.098765428589048465,"t":"re-directed to the muscles in our legs. In other words, sound stirs us at our biological","y":0.38953488356091337,"w":0.80041152954101558,"h":0.017441860778592888,"c":1},{"w":0.059670778910319008,"h":0.014534883982160851,"y":0.40988372065426848,"x":0.098765433610684114,"c":1,"t":"roots."},{"t":"A recent paper in Nature Neuroscience by a research team in Montreal, Canada, marks","x":0.098765432987820159,"y":0.43459302309431003,"w":0.82716049194335939,"h":0.017441860778592999,"c":1},{"h":0.016002068109590928,"t":"an important step in revealing the precise underpinnings of the potent pleasurable","w":0.77572016398111987,"y":0.45347467621737569,"c":1,"x":0.10082304550589243},{"c":1,"x":0.098765432937468728,"y":0.46937573598770521,"t":"stimulus' that is music. Although the study involves plenty of fancy technology, including","w":0.82716049194335928,"h":0.017667844522968212},{"h":0.0175171886933847,"c":1,"x":0.098765423283756024,"t":"functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) and ligand-based positron emission","w":0.77983540852864586,"y":0.48837209286220018},{"x":0.098681393853516486,"h":0.020824334646703413,"y":0.50477677220684602,"t":"tomography (PET) scanning, the experiment itself was rather straightforward. After","c":1,"w":0.78206949869791675},{"y":0.52210556434103039,"w":0.78829508463541653,"h":0.020471561643063518,"t":"screening 217 individuals who responded to advertisements requesting people who","x":0.10069834555020947,"c":1},{"w":0.8209876505533853,"y":0.54210836298619747,"c":1,"x":0.10082304706391682,"t":"experience 'chills' to instrumental music, the scientists narrowed down the subject pool","h":0.017484660423827014},{"t":"to ten. They then asked the subjects to bring in their playlist of favourite songs - virtually","y":0.55876428629678276,"w":0.83548767089843734,"h":0.018503784431304737,"c":1,"x":0.098715856025350018},{"x":0.1007484895853427,"h":0.019980631672170679,"c":1,"t":"every genre was represented, from techno to tango - and played them the music while","w":0.81909184773763022,"y":0.57681036790100548},{"w":0.83963968912760412,"x":0.09870897382028071,"t":"their brain activity was monitored. Because the scientists were combining methodologies","y":0.59429514267951178,"h":0.019728367404466041,"c":1},{"c":1,"w":0.82111775716145829,"t":"(PET and fMRI), they were able to obtain an impressively exact and detailed portrait of","h":0.018367187715671918,"x":0.098710688945473538,"y":0.61292151689691043},{"w":0.80864196777343755,"x":0.10082304906659904,"y":0.63074204920328347,"c":1,"t":"music in the brain. The first thing they discovered is that music triggers the production","h":0.01751376490149259},{"y":0.64680232573704932,"t":"of dopamine - a chemical with a key role in setting people's moods - by the neurons","x":0.10082307087171254,"w":0.80041147867838547,"h":0.018980948467277048,"c":1},{"t":"(nerve cells) in both the dorsal and ventral regions of the brain. As these two regions","w":0.7983539326985678,"c":1,"x":0.10082303357124961,"y":0.66715116264426411,"h":0.016005492463128768},{"h":0.018895348053517669,"x":0.10082303649154739,"t":"have long been linked with the experience of pleasure, this finding isn't particularly","y":0.68459302364210228,"w":0.77983540852864586,"c":1},{"y":0.70315760134722161,"w":0.10513626734415689,"h":0.016649913450854426,"x":0.10072404735585759,"c":1,"t":"surprising."},{"h":0.019946602685431913,"c":1,"y":0.72806958017277812,"w":0.73475575764973955,"t":"What is rather more significant is the finding that the dopamine neurons in the","x":0.1007384710726736},{"x":0.10082303450741316,"h":0.016007204359074767,"c":1,"t":"caudate - a region of the brain involved in learning stimulus-response associations,","y":0.74852767950278509,"w":0.79218109130859382},{"t":"and in anticipating food and other 'reward' stimuli - were at their most active around","w":0.79049575805664052,"h":0.022935207937575419,"x":0.10269329949186243,"y":0.76170100943119401,"c":1},{"x":0.10280291970540976,"w":0.8005555725097655,"t":"15 seconds before the participants'favourite moments in the music. The researchers","c":1,"y":0.78061712108190107,"h":0.019616079835925504},{"x":0.10288066592720166,"t":"call this the 'anticipatory phase' and argue that the purpose of this activity is to help","w":0.78395060221354151,"c":1,"h":0.017443572674538887,"y":0.80087209286280647},{"y":0.8197674417003854,"w":0.78395060221354185,"c":1,"x":0.10288066590164884,"h":0.017441860778592888,"t":"us predict the arrival of our favourite part. The question, of course, is what all these"},{"y":0.83627312352389427,"x":0.10279734114970665,"t":"dopamine neurons are up to. Why are they so active in the period preceding the","w":0.75531285603841147,"h":0.019661213680487455,"c":1},{"t":"acoustic climax? After all, we typically associate surges of dopamine with pleasure, with","w":0.82716049194335928,"c":1,"x":0.10288065933501041,"y":0.8545347469664325,"h":0.017667844522968212},{"w":0.80707102457682289,"t":"the processing of actual rewards. And yet, this cluster of cells is most active when the","y":0.86662287492772527,"h":0.02851037788166455,"x":0.10263864020521074,"c":1},{"t":"'chills' have yet to arrive, when the melodic pattern is still unresolved.","x":0.10082305521024192,"c":1,"h":0.017441860778592888,"w":0.65637858072916666,"y":0.89098837193351632},{"w":0.026748971939086902,"x":0.1028806579604977,"y":0.94331395332050527,"t":"66","h":0.013081395583944722,"c":1},{"t":"leltsresources.org","y":0.96768258536995222,"h":0.016454192297478354,"c":1,"x":0.39483721299237268,"w":0.20824633280436194}],"w":2400}